## Last In, First Out

A **stack** is a LIFO (Last In, First Out) data structure. The last item you push is the first item you pop — like a stack of plates where you can only take from the top.

### Building a Stack from a Vec

```xiom
type Stack[T] = {
  items: Vec[T];
}

fn Stack.new[T]() -> Stack[T] {
  return Stack[T]{ items: Vec[T].new() };
}

fn Stack.push[T](self: &mut Stack[T], item: T) {
  self.items.push(item);
}

fn Stack.pop[T](self: &mut Stack[T]) -> Option[T] {
  return self.items.pop();
}

fn Stack.peek[T](self) -> Option[T] {
  if self.items.len() == 0 {
    return None;
  }
  return self.items.get(self.items.len() - 1);
}

fn Stack.is_empty[T](self) -> Bool {
  return self.items.len() == 0;
}
```

### Push and Pop

`push` adds to the top. `pop` removes from the top. `peek` looks at the top without removing.

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var stack: Stack[Int] = Stack[Int].new();

  stack.push(&mut stack, 10);
  stack.push(&mut stack, 20);
  stack.push(&mut stack, 30);

  let top = stack.peek(&stack);
  io.println(match top { Some(v) => string.int_to_str(v), None => "empty" });

  let popped = stack.pop(&mut stack);
  io.println(match popped { Some(v) => string.int_to_str(v), None => "empty" });
  io.println(string.int_to_str(stack.is_empty(&stack) ? 1 : 0));
  return 0;
}
```

### Practical: Balanced Parentheses

A stack can check if parentheses are properly matched:

```xiom
fn is_balanced(code: &Vec[Str]) -> Bool {
  let stack: Stack[Str] = Stack[Str].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < code.len() {
    let ch = match code.get(i) { Some(c) => c, None => "" };
    if ch == "(" {
      stack.push(&mut stack, "(");
    } else if ch == ")" {
      if stack.is_empty(&stack) {
        return false;
      }
      stack.pop(&mut stack);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return stack.is_empty(&stack);
}
```

If the stack is empty at the end, every `(` had a matching `)`. This algorithm is used by every compiler and code editor you've ever used.

